  "_machines_note": "One machine a release, all of them the AnotherWorld system: the wizard's System list. The release setting is what the project records; the files (firmware) and the settings a release asks for follow from it. rawgl itself tells the releases apart by their files (Resource::detectVersion).",
  "author": "Delphine Software / Eric Chahi (1991); rawgl by Gregory Montoir (cyx); chimera port by Sergio Martin",
  "url": "https://github.com/ToolAssisted-run/chimera-core-rawgl",
  "deterministic": true,
  "_memory_note": "[sbrk, sealed, invisible, plain, mmap] MiB: the engine's 1 MiB of game memory and its picture pages on the heap; the game's files, unpacked from the project's zip, in sealed memory - the anniversary editions' data and a SoundFont's samples (as floats, twice the file) are the large ones; a 3DO disc image is read in place and takes none; the engine's own stack (1 MiB), the decoders' buffers and a SoundFont while it loads, mmap'd. To be sized to the real releases' data once they are measured.",
  "memoryLayoutMiB": [
    64,
    1024,
    4,
    4,
    512
  ],
  "video": {
    "_comment": "The game's 320x200 screen, drawn by rawgl's software renderer - as the 1991 original drew it for the DOS, Amiga, Atari ST and Windows 3.1 releases and the anniversary editions (whose HD pictures are rawgl's OpenGL renderer's and are not drawn), in 15-bit colour for the 3DO - shown on a 4:3 monitor. The 3DO's and Windows 3.1's full-screen pictures (logos, title, the pause) are shown at their own size, up to 640x480: the live size comes from GetVideoWidth/Height.",
    "width": 640,
    "height": 480,
    "virtualWidth": 320,
    "virtualHeight": 240,
    "vsyncNumerator": 25,
    "vsyncDenominator": 2,
    "_vsync_note": "A frame is one step of the game (docs/game-cores.md): one frame the game shows, held for as many fiftieths of a second as its script says (VAR_PAUSE_SLICES; most of the game 4, 12.5 Hz), sixtieths on the 3DO; the pause, each 50 ms it waits for P. GetVsyncNumerator/Denominator report the step just run (1000/milliseconds); 25/2 is four fiftieths.",
    "getBgra": "GetVideoBgra"
  },
  "audio": {
    "_comment": "The game's sound as rawgl's SDL build plays it, at 44100 Hz for exactly the time each step covers: the engine's own channels and module player (DOS, Amiga, Atari ST), whose music tells the script where it is (VAR_MUSIC_SYNC) and so plays by the machine's time; the anniversary editions' and Windows 3.1's WAV sounds with their music (a WAV, an Ogg Vorbis, a MIDI file played with the SoundFont firmware, when the soundFont setting asks for it); the 3DO's AIFF sounds and SDX2 songs.",
    "rate": 44100,
    "samplesPerFrame": 44200,
    "channels": 2,
    "get": "GetAudio"
  },
  "lag": {
    "inputWasRead": "InputWasRead"
  },
  "input": {
    "name": "Another World",
    "_comment": "The game's controls as rawgl reads them: the joystick (the arrows; Up jumps, Down crouches - on the 3DO, Jump jumps) and its fire button (run, shoot, charge the gun; Space or Enter), then the keyboard's commands - Code (C, to the password screen, where a release has one) and Pause (P, not on the copy protection or the intro) - and the letters and Backspace, which only the password screen reads. A button pressed is a key going down, a button let go a key coming up, as rawgl's SDL frontend sees them: where the game takes a press (a menu, a picture it waits on), a button still held is not pressed again until it is let go. Jump is the 3DO's only (IsButtonActive). The 3DO's Back key (its end menu) is not offered. Left out: rawgl's screenshot and fast-mode keys, which are the frontend's business.",
    "buttons": [
      "P1 Up",
      "P1 Down",
      "P1 Left",
      "P1 Right",
      "P1 Action",
      "P1 Jump",
      "Code",
      "Pause",
      "Key A",
      "Key B",
      "Key C",
      "Key D",
      "Key E",
      "Key F",
      "Key G",
      "Key H",
      "Key I",
      "Key J",
      "Key K",
      "Key L",
      "Key M",
      "Key N",
      "Key O",
      "Key P",
      "Key Q",
      "Key R",
      "Key S",
      "Key T",
      "Key U",
      "Key V",
      "Key W",
      "Key X",
      "Key Y",
      "Key Z",
      "Key Backspace"
    ],
    "mnemonics": {
      "Up": "U",
      "Down": "D",
      "Left": "L",
      "Right": "R",
      "Action": "F",
      "Jump": "J",
      "Code": "C",
      "Pause": "P",
      "Key A": "a",
      "Key B": "b",
      "Key C": "c",
      "Key D": "d",
      "Key E": "e",
      "Key F": "f",
      "Key G": "g",
      "Key H": "h",
